# Decouple Run capture auth from Typesense

## Failure

Flagg Typesense needed 57 minutes to rebuild 24 collections on 2026-07-29.
`machines_dev` contained only four Machine records and loaded within seconds, but
Typesense returned HTTP 503 for every collection until the complete rebuild
finished. Run capture authenticated each request through `machines_dev`, so an
unrelated 241,939-document `docs_chunks_v2` rebuild made capture authentication
unavailable.

The Pi `memory-capture` extension now returns immediately and bounds its delivery
worker, but Central must also keep its request path independent of a non-critical
search projection.

## Decision

Central stores the current Machine token hashes and identities in
`~/.joelclaw/capture-auth.db`, mode `0600`.

- Known tokens resolve from local SQLite without a network call.
- `machines_dev` remains the enrollment and migration mirror.
- The worker refreshes the complete four-row registry every five minutes outside
  the request path.
- An unknown token gets one bounded Typesense lookup. A successful lookup is
  persisted locally.
- A failed, malformed, or empty sync keeps the last working registry.
- Revocation and token rotation replace the local Machine row on the next
  successful background sync.

This preserves every existing Flagg/Blaine token. Signed capture capability
tokens remain a possible later replacement for the PDS App Password minting
flow, but they are not required to remove Typesense from the request path.

## Verification

- Seven focused auth tests pass: persistence across reopen, zero-call offline
  lookup, bounded migration fallback, revocation, empty-sync preservation,
  malformed records, and timeout behavior.
- The broader Run route and health set passes: 19 tests total.
- A live temporary registry synchronized all four `machines_dev` records.
- The current Flagg token resolved after reopening the registry with a fetch
  implementation that always failed.
- The SQLite file was `0600`.
- Commit `b3149dc2` was pushed to `main` and fast-forwarded onto Blaine.
- Flagg restarted only the system-bus worker. Health returned HTTP 200 and
  reported `captureAuth.backend: "sqlite"`.
- The worker synchronized four Machine identities at startup.
- A Flagg capture canary returned HTTP 202 in 10.5 ms.
- The production registry resolved Flagg's token in 0.2 ms while its injected
  fetch implementation failed. It made zero remote calls.
- A capture canary sent from Blaine returned HTTP 202 in 14.3 ms.
- Blaine's live Pi extension symlink points at the patched non-blocking
  `memory-capture` package.

## Non-goal

This change does not make Typesense rebuild its in-memory indexes faster.
Typesense 30.2 has no collection lazy-load mode. The remaining boot-time work is
to retire dead derived collections or move archive-sized indexes to a separate
non-critical process. Those collection drops require inventory and explicit
review because they are destructive.

## Pi extension follow-up

The 2026-07-29 fleet audit found two independent `turn_end` blockers.

- `memory-capture` used to return its network delivery promise to Pi. It now
  schedules capture without blocking the lifecycle handler and bounds HTTP work.
- Blaine's `pi-codex-computer-use` awaited an MCP notification with no timeout
  after every connected turn. Its `turn_end` handler is now fire-and-forget,
  connection timers are cleared, and shutdown is bounded to two seconds.

The computer-use patch is commit `a66625f` in the local source checkout. The
ShitRat GitHub App cannot access that repository, so the push is blocked. The
patched files were copied to Blaine after the remote source hash matched the
expected parent revision. Blaine passed the syntax check and two lifecycle
tests.

Blaine also loaded the retired `pi-rhizomatic` package. Its first-agent hook
waited on two calls to a parked service. No Rhizomatic tool calls appeared in
269 Pi sessions from the prior 45 days. The package was removed from Blaine's
active Pi settings, while its source and a timestamped settings backup remain.
Offline Pi startup on Blaine fell from 1.70 seconds to 0.99 seconds.

The zoo is large but not uniformly dead. In the same 45-day Blaine sample,
computer-use appeared 14 times, MCP 3 times, Bellwether's Herdr tools 2 times,
subagents 260 times, Pi Notes 350 times, and browser/search tools 1,164 times.
Keep those. The retired Rhizomatic package had evidence for removal; the others
do not.

## Memory backlog recovery

The capture request path is healthy after the worker restart. A valid token
reaches request validation without Typesense, and a real Blaine backlog blob
returned HTTP 202. The local session index also passes `PRAGMA quick_check`;
recent count and lookup queries complete in 10 ms.

The old clients left a prefix-explosion backlog:

- Flagg: 1,064 JSON blobs, 1.9 GiB; 978 are older than seven days.
- Blaine: 1,165 JSON blobs, 16.8 GB; 1,162 are older than seven days.
- The patched clients coalesce one pending body per source cursor, so this old
  prefix explosion no longer repeats.

Recovery is complete without blind resends.

- The replay planner collapses exact and growing-prefix duplicates.
- It verifies indexed bytes against `sessions.db` and the raw Run store.
- It reconstructs both current source-cursor segments and legacy parent Run
  chains.
- Blaine collapsed to 26 representatives. All 26 are indexed.
- Flagg collapsed to 153 representatives. The worker indexed 88 replay items,
  including one deterministic child after a retained-prefix collision. All 153
  are now indexed.
- Flagg's 1,064 reviewed files moved to
  `~/.joelclaw/outbox-quarantine/20260729T2355Z`.
- Blaine's 1,165 reviewed files moved to
  `~/.joelclaw/outbox-quarantine/20260729T2327Z`.
- Both active outboxes are empty. The quarantine files still consume 1.9 GiB
  and 16 GB respectively; they have not been deleted.

Private frozen copies and SHA-bearing catalogs remain under
`~/.joelclaw/backups/capture-outbox-{flagg,blaine}/`. The quarantine tool only
moves a live file when its size and SHA match the reviewed catalog. Missing,
new, or changed files stay in the active outbox.

`joelclaw recall` is healthy and returned three SQLite FTS5 hits in 130 ms. Its
health envelope incorrectly called the projection stale because the retired,
intentionally frozen May 31 `memory_observations` archive was included in the
live freshness gate. The code now requires that archive for availability and
integrity, but only age-gates live Brain, observation, vault, and knowledge
sources.
